        en: "Did you know that the red coral jewellery offered to you in Tabarka is bound up with the city's history? Since antiquity this precious material has attracted those coveting the “red gold of the Mediterranean”, as coral is sometimes called. It resembles a plant bearing small white flowers, but coral is in fact a calcified skeleton inhabited by tiny animals of the sea-anemone and jellyfish family. Red coral has become rare today, though it was once abundant, especially on Tunisia's northern coast. In the sixteenth century Genoese merchants secured a monopoly over it and, to protect their business, built a fortified village at Tabarka, of which the Genoese fort — the city's emblem — still stands. Today one must dive to great depths to find it.",
